-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THIRD-PARTY ATTRIBUTIONS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) TUI visual tokens (colors / symbols / spinner / diff palette / logo)
   Location: tuan_cli/ui/theme.py, tuan_cli/tui/tui.py
   Origin:   design tokens taken from the `src/tui` of Kimi Code
   Upstream: https://github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-code
   Holder:   Moonshot AI
   License:  MIT (text reproduced below)

   Note: the visual tokens (colors, symbols, spinner frames, diff palette,
   ASCII logo) are reused as-is under the MIT license above.  All functional
   logic in the TUI (command completion, audit panel, status bar, mascot
   state machine, trust-confirmation flow) is independently implemented by
   the Tuan-CLI project.

2) Protocol layer (ports/protocol/)
   Location: tuan_cli/ports/protocol/ (13 modules)
   Origin:   ported from the upstream TypeScript reference implementation
   Upstream: https://github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-code
   License:  MIT

   Note: the protocol types, envelope, error-codes, events, and pagination
   logic are independently written in Python, aligned to the upstream's
   public JSON-RPC / NDJSON message formats for interoperability.

3) OAuth / identity layer (ports/oauth/)
   Location: tuan_cli/ports/oauth/ (9 modules)
   Origin:   ported from the upstream TypeScript reference implementation
   Upstream: https://github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-code
   License:  MIT

   Note: the device-identity, token-state, error-classification, and
   custom-registry logic are independently written in Python.  The platform
   identifier (`kimi_code_cli`) and User-Agent format follow the upstream
   convention for OAuth interoperability with Moonshot AI endpoints; these
   can be overridden via `KIMI_CODE_CUSTOM_HEADERS` or by supplying a
   different `KimiHostIdentity`.

4) Migration-legacy layer (ports/migration_legacy/)
   Location: tuan_cli/ports/migration_legacy/ (16 modules)
   Origin:   ported from the upstream TypeScript reference implementation
   Upstream: https://github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-code
   License:  MIT

   Note: the parsers for legacy kimi-cli persisted state (sessions,
   metadata, classification) are independently written in Python, aligned
   to the upstream's on-disk JSON format for backward-compatible migration.

5) Transcript / media-ref layer (ports/transcript/)
   Location: tuan_cli/ports/transcript/
   Origin:   ported from the upstream TypeScript reference implementation
   Upstream: https://github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-code
   License:  MIT

   Note: the `kimi-file://` URI scheme and media-path-tag parsing are
   retained for interoperability with upstream-produced transcripts.  The
   implementation is independently written in Python.

6) Telemetry layer (ports/telemetry/)
   Location: tuan_cli/ports/telemetry/
   Origin:   ported from the upstream TypeScript reference implementation
   Upstream: https://github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-code
   License:  MIT

   Note: the event serialization, enrichment, and transport-abstraction
   logic are independently written in Python.  The `TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT`
   constant mirrors the upstream's default endpoint for compatibility;
   actual telemetry delivery uses the OpenTelemetry OTLP path (see
   `ports/telemetry/otel.py`) and defaults to `localhost:4318`.

7) Kaos / environment layer (ports/kaos/)
   Location: tuan_cli/ports/kaos/
   Origin:   ported from the upstream TypeScript reference implementation
   Upstream: https://github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-code
   License:  MIT

   Note: the agent-operating-system interface (login, paths, environment
   detection) is independently written in Python, aligned to the upstream's
   public interface for interoperability.

8) Ecosystem interop defaults (config/defaults.py)
   Location: tuan_cli/config/defaults.py (ecosystem section)
   Origin:   Claude Code and Hermes Agent project structures
   Upstream: https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code, https://github.com/nousresearch/hermes-agent
   License:  respective licenses

   Note: by default, Tuan-CLI scans `~/.claude/` and `~/.hermes/` for
   skills, named agents, and memories when `ecosystem.claude_code.enabled`
   or `ecosystem.hermes.enabled` is `True`.  Users can disable these in
   `~/.tuan/config.yaml` to prevent any access to third-party CLI data.

